[38;5;231m⏺[39m I'm treating this as a bounded task. The page, the data and the render loop already exist, and filtering is a change
  to that flow. So I'll put a short design here in chat rather than write a spec, and I won't start building until you
  approve it.

  The first question is where the controls go, which is easier to see than to read, so I've opened a browser tab with
  three wireframes. If it didn't open, use:
  [94m]8;id=cwktlh;http://localhost:52930/?key=a6fd10018e8e4a1ed194fd9d916bdcd61de55ce780ada9937f2fe82d090013d4\http://localhost:52930/?key=a6fd10018e8e4a1ed194fd9d916bdcd61de55ce780ada9937f2fe82d090013d4[39m]8;;\ (it uses a fair number of
  tokens, so say if you'd rather stay in the terminal).

  All three layouts use the same three controls: an event-type Select, a search over device, location and details, and a
  "Failed sign-ins only" quick filter.

  - [1mA. Toolbar row between the intro and the table.[0m Every control is visible at once, and the fields stack on narrow
    screens. This is the smallest diff.
  - [1mB. A "Filters" button that opens a popover, with applied filters shown as removable tags.[0m It's more compact, but
    reaching the controls takes an extra click and the popover adds focus-handling work.
  - [1mC. A filter panel beside the table.[0m This squeezes a six-column table into a 960px page, and on narrow screens it has
    to fall back to A anyway.

  I recommend A. The support tickets were all people hunting for one failed sign-in, and A puts that checkbox in plain
  sight.

  Take a look and tell me what you think here. You can also click an option in the browser to select it.

  The companion saves its mockups in [38;5;153m.hyperpowers/[39m, which isn't in [38;5;153m.gitignore[39m yet. I won't commit anything under it.

